 # Registry Design
 
-## Context
+## Context and Design Philosophy
 
 Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest.
 
+The registry is an offline, read-only projection of centrally managed inventory data. It favors deterministic local behavior and preservation of the last valid snapshot over minimizing per-kiosk import work. SQLite is an implementation detail of this node; consumers depend only on the lookup result contract.
+
 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. A found record contains the equipment description, calibration date, and due date. The certificates node uses only this interface and cannot access registry storage or SQLite types.
+
+Snapshot validation, index preparation, activation, and lookup all belong to this node. Each kiosk builds an embedded SQLite database from an accepted snapshot and serves lookups from that local database. Registry operation requires no service process, network route, credentials, or external search endpoint.
+
+## Local Storage
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+The registry keeps one active SQLite database and prepares replacements as separate staging databases in the same application-owned directory. The database stores snapshot metadata once and equipment records in a table keyed by normalized serial number. The serial key has a unique constraint, making the database both the lookup index and a final guard against ambiguous records.
+
+The active database is immutable after activation. Lookups open it read-only. Temporary import files and inactive staging databases are not lookup candidates and may be removed after a failed import or during startup cleanup.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Treat the manifest, snapshot data, and signature as one import unit. Before indexing records, verify the signature with the bundled public key and reject unsupported schema versions. Stream the newline-delimited records into a new staging database in one transaction, validating required fields and normalized serial-number uniqueness as they are inserted.
+
+Commit the staging transaction only after the complete input has been read and its record count and digest agree with the signed manifest. Run a database integrity check, close the staging database, and then publish it as the active database with an atomic filesystem replacement in the application-owned directory. A lookup opened before activation may finish against the previous immutable database; lookups opened after activation use the replacement. No lookup can observe a partially populated database.
+
+Any signature, schema, manifest, record, uniqueness, database, or activation failure aborts the import, removes the staging database when possible, and reports the failing stage without replacing the active database. The snapshot payload is untrusted input and is never used to construct SQL identifiers or statements.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Apply the same normalization while importing records, and reject a snapshot if two source serial numbers produce the same normalized key. Serial-number punctuation and internal whitespace remain significant.
+
+Query the active database by exact equality on its indexed normalized serial key. Return `Found(record, snapshotDate)` for one match and `NotFound(snapshotDate)` for no match. The snapshot date comes from the same active database as the records, including for `NotFound`, so activation cannot combine records and metadata from different snapshots.
+
+If no valid snapshot has ever been activated, lookup cannot produce the normal `Found` or `NotFound` result because neither would carry a valid snapshot date. This startup state remains an interface decision to resolve before requirements are written.
+
+## Recovery and Concurrency
+
+At startup, ignore and clean up staging artifacts; they are never promoted implicitly. Open only the database at the active path and verify that its schema and snapshot metadata are readable before accepting lookups. If the active database cannot be opened or verified, report the registry as unavailable rather than returning `NotFound`.
+
+Imports are serialized so that only one staging database can be prepared at a time. Lookups continue against the immutable active database during preparation. Activation is a short publish operation after all expensive validation and indexing work has completed.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup deployment | Embedded SQLite index built on each kiosk | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Local lookup satisfies the offline-only architecture and gives predictable behavior without a service, credentials, or network timeout path. Per-kiosk validation and rebuild work remains contained within the registry node. |
+| Snapshot publication | Build a separate immutable database and atomically replace the active database | Rebuild the active database in place; update rows incrementally | Separate preparation keeps partial imports invisible and preserves the last valid snapshot on validation or build failure. |
+| Lookup key | Unique normalized serial key in SQLite | Scan snapshot records; maintain a separate index file | The database constraint enforces the same uniqueness rule used by exact indexed lookup without a second derived artifact. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Define the registry interface result when no valid snapshot has ever been activated.
+2. Decide whether a correctly signed snapshot older than the active snapshot date may replace it.
+3. Define the recovery behavior if the active database is unreadable and a previously valid database is still present.
